{"name":"Misty Blue","hex":"#b4c4c3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"misty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered in mist; foggy.","Dim; vague; obscure."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/misty"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Misty Blue","blocks":[{"text":"\"Misty Blue\" is a song written by Bob Montgomery that has been recorded and made commercially successful by several music artists. Although Montgomery wrote the song for a different artist in mind, it was brought first to the attention of Wilma Burgess in 1966. It was recorded by Eddy Arnold the following year, both versions were top five country hits. A decade later, blues artist Dorothy Moore released the highest-charting version of the song and it reached the top ten in several different radio formats. Following Moore's revival of the track, numerous artists re-covered the tune, including country artist Billie Jo Spears. Spears's version would also go on to become a successful single release. Numerous other artists and musicians of different genres have recorded their own versions of \"Misty Blue\". The song is now considered both a country music and blues standard."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/25/Wilma_Burgess--Misty_Blue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Misty_Blue","wikidata":"Q6880516"},{"title":"Misty Blue (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Misty Blue is a 1968 studio album by the American jazz singer Ella Fitzgerald, featuring mostly renditions of recent country music hits. The single \"I Taught Him Everything He Knows\" reached Billboard's Easy Listening chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b0/Mistyblue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Misty_Blue_(album)","wikidata":"Q2752696"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c7/Misty_Blue_-_Eddy_Arnold.jpg/250px-Misty_Blue_-_Eddy_Arnold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c7/Misty_Blue_-_Eddy_Arnold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c7/Misty_Blue_-_Eddy_Arnold.jpg/250px-Misty_Blue_-_Eddy_Arnold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMisty_Blue_-_Eddy_Arnold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org